Measured: /framework 59 inbound internal links, /papers 39, /guides 34. The
authority tier is the largest accumulation of link equity on this site and
it is doing real work.

/estimate had SIX inbound links in the entire codebase, and not one of them
was from a paper, a guide or a glossary term.

The 47 glossary entries linked to exactly four things: the homepage, the
glossary index, a framework pillar, and their source paper. Nothing
commercial on any of them. A reader who had just worked out why their floor
cupped had nowhere to go.

The technical papers were worse: no commercial link of any kind. Not a
service, not the estimate form, not even the homepage anchor the rest of the
tier uses. The second-highest inbound link count on the site, routing
nowhere at all.

WHY FIVE ENTRIES AND NOT FORTY-SEVEN

The obvious version of lib/authority-routes.ts maps every term to a service.
That is forty-seven judgements I am not qualified to make and nobody would
ever check — and a wrong one sends somebody with a refinishing question to an
installation page, which is worse than sending them nowhere.

So it maps the SOURCE PAPER. Every glossary term already declares which paper
it came from, that mapping was made by somebody who knows the trade, and
there are five papers. Each entry carries the sentence justifying where it
points, so a person who disagrees argues with prose rather than guessing at
an intention. A paper with no mapping renders no block — silence is the right
output for "we do not know what this person needs", and a test asserts the
function returns null rather than guessing.

The papers and the glossary read the same mapping, so the two tiers cannot
disagree about what a subject bears on.

TWO GUARDS PUSHED BACK. BOTH WERE INSTRUCTIVE.

`pnpm seo:density` failed: /guides/hardwood-flooring-cost-toronto "reaches 0
cta, needs 1". Twice, for two different reasons.

First because the guard counts `/#quote` and did not count `/estimate` — it
predates the estimate route being the preferred target, so moving a CTA from
a homepage fragment onto the page built for it made a compliant page fail.
WIDENED AND DISCLOSED: every href that counted before still counts, and
`/estimate` is added. Nothing is newly rejected.

Then it failed again, because I had written `href={ESTIMATE_HREF}` through an
exported constant. The guard extracts LITERAL href strings. So the link
existed, the page was correct, and the guard reported zero. A constant that
hides a link from a link guard is a constant that should not exist — it is
deleted, `/estimate` is written literally at every call site where the guard
and a reader can both see it, and the reasoning is in the file where the
constant used to be.

That is the second time today a piece of my own indirection defeated a real
check. It is worth the pattern being named: readable abstraction over a
string that a static analyser reads is a trade, not a free improvement.

Verified: 65 of 66 guards. verify:css needs postcss from node_modules, absent
here. seo:density now passes — "every commercial page reaches services,
guides, evidence, method, standard and a CTA". parse-scan 618 files, zero
diagnostics. Resolving typecheck clean. 35 new assertions.

